<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Howard Florey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Howard Florey was an Australian pathologist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>, who led the team at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> that turned penicillin into a usable medicine. With Ernst Chain and others he purified the drug and showed its power against bacterial infection in 1940 and 1941, and its mass production saved many lives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in 1945 with Chain and Alexander Fleming.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/howard-florey.jpg" alt="Howard Florey" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Smith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Howard_Florey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
